Q: How can I (portably) determine the start time of program? A: <p>Another imperfect approach is using some compilers' support for marking code as intended to run before a program's <code>main()</code>.</p>
<p>GCC's C++ compiler offers <code>__attribute__((constructor))</code>, and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/23637301/1593077">so does LLVM's</a>. So,</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;

std::chrono::time_point&lt;std::chrono::system_clock, std::chrono::nanoseconds&gt;
program_start_time() noexcept
{
    static const auto start_time = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
    return start_time;
}

__attribute__((constructor))
static void ensure_start_time_initialization() {
    program_start_time();
}
</code></pre>
<p>combines @AndreyTurkin's suggestion with a forced initialization before <code>main()</code> execution starts. You still don't get the <em>exact</em> program start time, but it's reasonably close, plus - the exact definition of when a program is considered to have started is a a matter of some disagreement.</p>
<p>Anyway, you get portability of the solution across platforms 'for free', if you're using  these two compilers. It seems Microsoft's MSVC also supports a similar mechanism, although <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/2390626/1593077">it is a bit hairier</a>. And with these three compilers, that's pretty wide support. Other C++ compilers may well have their own mechanism for achieving this.</p>
<p><sub>PS - Remember that the system clock is a very fickle thing, since an admin can change it while your program is running, or the timezone may change, or Daylight Savings Time may start or end etc. So make sure that's what you want rather than, say, the steady clock. </sub></p>
